.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt CHROOT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm chroot
.Nd change the root directory
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn chroot "const char *path"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn chroot
makes the directory
.Fa path
names the root directory of the calling process.
The process's absolute paths start there, and so does the target of a
symbolic link that begins with
.Ql / ;
.Ql ..
in the root stays in the root.
.Fa path
itself is resolved as any path is, from the old root or from the working
directory.
.Pp
The root belongs to the process, and its threads share it, as they share
the working directory.
.Xr fork 2
gives the child a copy of the parent's;
.Xr execve 2
keeps it, and the program it runs is found from it.
While a process has its root in a directory,
.Xr rmdir 2
refuses the directory, and
.Xr umount2 2
refuses the mount that holds it.
.Pp
The working directory stays where it is when it is inside the new root,
and
.Xr getcwd 2
then reports its path from the new root.
One outside the new root moves to the new root.
On Linux it stays where it was, and relative paths then reach the tree
outside the root.
In the same way, a descriptor of a directory outside the root, opened
before the call, is refused with
.Er ENOENT
by
.Xr fchdir 2
and by the
.Fn *at
calls for a relative path, where Linux walks from that directory.
.Pp
Only the root of the mount table has
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /proc
below it.
A program inside another root reaches a device through a device node
made with
.Xr mknod 2
inside the root, which opens the
.Pa /dev
device with its number.
A directory of
.Pa /dev
or
.Pa /proc
cannot be a root.
.Xr mount 2
takes its source by its name under
.Pa /dev
whatever the caller's root, where Linux resolves the name from the root.
.Fn chroot
of
.Pa /
from the root of the mount table changes nothing.
.Pp
The i386 system call number is 61.
The kernel takes the path in EBX.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn chroot
returns 0 on success, or -1 on failure and sets
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Pa tests/musl/chrootmusl.c
chroots into a directory of the KRFFS root and one of a tmpfs.
It checks that the new root's files are found by absolute paths and
nothing above it is, that
.Ql ..
stays at the root, that absolute and climbing symbolic links resolve
inside it, that the working directory keeps its path from the new root
or moves to it, that a caller who may not search its root is refused
.Fn chdir \(dq/\(dq ,
and that a child of
.Xr fork 2
and a program run by
.Xr execve 2
keep the root.
It also checks that
.Xr rmdir 2
and
.Xr umount2 2
are refused while a process has its root there.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENAMETOOLONG]"
.It Bq Er EACCES
A directory of
.Fa path ,
the last one included, does not grant search permission.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa path
is outside the caller's memory.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa path
names a directory of
.Pa /dev
or
.Pa /proc ,
whose file systems take the rest of a path in one step, so the resolver
could not stop
.Ql ..
there.
Linux has no such refusal.
.It Bq Er ELOOP
Too many symbolic links were followed.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
.Fa path
is too long.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
A directory of
.Fa path
does not exist, or
.Fa path
is empty.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel had no memory for the path.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
.Fa path ,
or a component of it, is not a directory.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller's effective user ID is not 0.
The errors above come first, as on Linux.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chdir 2 ,
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr mknod 2 ,
.Xr mount 2 ,
.Xr rmdir 2
